Test alfe's `--mode automation' on macOS, like we do on Windows.

pjb, 2026-08-01: "Next I think we should test --automation on macos
like on windows."


* Why

alfe has two ways to drive BricsCAD:

  --mode batch       the GUI executable + a script:
                     bricscad.exe [/Automation] /b run.scr   (Windows)
                     bricscad -B run.scr                     (macOS/Linux)

  --mode automation  an out-of-process launcher script:
                     cscript //nologo bridge-bricscad.vbs    (Windows, COM)
                     osascript launcher.applescript          (macOS)

The Windows automation path has been driven against the real product
many times (it is where BRICSCAD_COM_MODE, bridge-vbs.log and the
SendCommand timing work came from). The macOS counterpart —
EMIT-LAUNCHER-APPLESCRIPT + osascript — has NEVER been exercised end
to end: no CI job runs it, and no manual run is recorded. We do not
know whether it reaches READY at all.

Note this is alfe's own mode flag, distinct from BricsCAD's
`/Automation' executable switch (the hidden-main-frame startup switch
added for alfe-bricscad-batch-hidden-ui, Windows-only, used inside
--mode batch).


* Test job (prepared, awaiting a runner)

  scripts/verify-bricscad-automation.sh
  .gitlab-ci.yml : verify:bricscad-automation:macos   (manual, allow_failure)

Phase 1 runs the minimal request in --mode automation on the real
BricsCAD and requires the whole cycle: READY -> request -> DONE ->
clean exit, with the marker echoed back.

Phase 2 runs ONLY if phase 1 fails: the same request in --mode batch,
as a control. That splits the two failure stories:

  automation FAIL + batch PASS  -> the macOS automation path is at fault
  automation FAIL + batch FAIL  -> the engine or the runner is at fault

Both phases log the argv via alfe --print-command, so the osascript /
bricscad command line appears in the trace even when the launch never
gets off the ground. A failing phase keeps its workdir and publishes
launcher.applescript, run-common.lsp, run.scr and protocol/*.txt as
job artifacts (dist/bricscad-automation/).


* Acceptance

- verify:bricscad-automation:macos runs GREEN on the macOS CAD runner
  (thalassa), i.e. --mode automation completes the protocol cycle; or
- it fails and we have the artefacts to say precisely why, at which
  point this issue becomes the fix ticket for the AppleScript
  launcher.

A Windows counterpart job (verify:bricscad-automation:windows, driving
the COM/VBScript bridge) is worth adding for symmetry once the macOS
side has a verdict.


* First run: FAIL, and now we know why (2026-08-01)

verify:bricscad-automation:macos, job 15659895556, thalassa, real
BricsCAD V26:

  ### phase 1: --mode automation (the path under test)
  == [automation] command line alfe would launch ==
  osascript /var/folders/.../alfe-bricscad-81366-y58ksi/launcher.applescript
  alfe[warn]: backend BRICSCAD: READY timeout after 240.01 s; last status = "BOOTING"
  == [automation] FAIL: exit=4 marker-present=0 ==

  ### phase 2: --mode batch (control)
  '/Applications/BricsCAD V26.app/Contents/MacOS/bricscad' '/Users/pjb/.../Default-mm.dwt' -B /var/folders/.../run.scr
  AUTOMATION-PROBE-OK-81364
  == [batch] PASS (exit 0, marker echoed) ==

  ### RESULT: automation FAIL / batch PASS

The control did its job: BricsCAD and the runner are healthy, and the
fault is in the macOS automation path alone. status.txt never left
BOOTING and protocol/stdout.txt and stderr.txt were EMPTY — the CAD
never received the (load ...) at all.

** Cause: the emitted AppleScript could not run

Two independent faults in *BRICSCAD-APPLESCRIPT-TEMPLATE*, either of
which aborts the script before a single keystroke is sent:

  1. `set running to exists (processes whose name is "bricscad")'
     — `running' is a RESERVED AppleScript property term, so this is a
     compile error, not an assignment.

  2. `tell application "BricsCAD" to activate' — the installed bundle
     is "BricsCAD V26.app"; there is no application named "BricsCAD".

That is consistent with every observable: empty channels, status stuck
at BOOTING, and the CAD never started.

** Why it cost 240 s instead of failing at once

alfe waited out the whole READY timeout because the READY wait ignored
the launcher process. osascript had already exited non-zero, and its
exit code and stderr — the actual diagnosis — were discarded.
WAIT-FOR-STATUS-PREFIX has always accepted :ALIVE-P; BricsCAD's
START-ENGINE simply never passed it.

** Fixed (alfe 1.7.11)

  - Template: launch-or-activate in one step with
    `do shell script "open -a " & quoted form of appPath', addressing
    the REAL bundle (MACOS-APP-BUNDLE-FOR derives it from the
    discovered binary). No reserved word, no guessed app name, no
    process-name probing. Startup delay 2.0 -> 8.0 s (a cold start on
    the runner takes ~9 s), overridable with
    $ALFE_BRICSCAD_STARTUP_DELAY.
  - SUBSTITUTE-PLACEHOLDERS took an :ESCAPE function. It had always
    applied VBS-ESCAPE — right for the VBScript bridge, wrong here: it
    doubled the quotes of the injected AppleScript fragment. Caught by
    the new unit tests, not by CI.
  - START-ENGINE now passes :ALIVE-P. A launcher that exits NON-ZERO
    aborts the wait immediately with a :LAUNCHER-FAILED error carrying
    its exit code and stderr; a launcher that exits 0 is a driver that
    finished its job (osascript after typing, cscript after
    SendCommand) and the wait continues, so the batch path is
    unchanged.

** Still open: does the keystroke approach work in CI at all?

Even with a correct script, `keystroke' needs an interactive GUI
session AND Accessibility (TCC) permission for the process running
osascript. A GitLab shell runner typically has neither, in which case
System Events raises -25211 and osascript exits non-zero — which alfe
will now report immediately and precisely instead of hanging.

Re-run the job. Three outcomes, three different next steps:

  - PASS                      -> macOS automation works; close.
  - :LAUNCHER-FAILED -25211   -> permission/session limit, not a code
                                 bug. Decide: grant Accessibility to
                                 the runner, or declare --mode
                                 automation unsupported on macOS and
                                 make it fail fast with that message
                                 instead of pretending.
  - :LAUNCHER-FAILED other    -> read the reported stderr; script bug.

Either way the 240 s silent hang is gone.
